(Chapter 1: Cognitive Foundations of Active Recall) Tj
/F1 10 Tf
0 -24 Td
(Active recall is a learning principle that enhances long-term retention by actively stimulating memory during the study process. Unlike passive review techniques such as rereading or highlighting, active retrieval forces the brain to reconstruct knowledge pathways, strengthening synaptic connections and consolidating memory traces.) Tj
0 -20 Td
(This paragraph expands upon the analytical implications for curricular design, emphasizing structured dialectical interrogation over superficial memorization.) Tj
ET

endstream
